    plainEnglish:`Think of your appetite as a construction site that keeps requesting more materials — whether it needs them or not.\n\nYour body has two completely separate approval chains that are supposed to cut off those requests when the site is fully stocked. One runs through the gut. One runs through the pancreas. Both report independently to the project manager — your brain — and both need to check in for the "stop ordering" signal to be strong enough.\n\nFor most people, only one of those approval chains is functioning. This stack activates both.\n\n**Semaglutide — The Warehouse Sign-Off**\n\nAfter you eat, your gut releases a hormone called GLP-1 that tells the project manager "the warehouse is stocked, cancel the next delivery." Semaglutide mimics that hormone — but stays active far longer than the real thing.\n\nIt's the warehouse manager who keeps the sign-off active all week instead of clocking out after a few hours.\n\n**Cagrilintide — The Logistics Sign-Off**\n\nYour pancreas runs a completely separate approval chain using a different hormone called amylin. It reaches the project manager through entirely different channels — amylin receptors — and sends its own independent "we have enough, stop the orders" report.\n\nCagrilintide mimics amylin. Same outcome, completely different approval chain. When both sign-offs arrive at the project manager's desk simultaneously, the stop signal is roughly 3x stronger than either one alone.\n\n| Compound | Role |\n|---|---|\n| Semaglutide | Warehouse sign-off — gut's approval chain via GLP-1 |\n| Cagrilintide | Logistics sign-off — pancreas's approval chain via amylin |\n\n---\n\nThe project manager isn't getting a louder version of the same report. It's getting two completely independent sign-offs from two different departments at the same time. That's why CagriSema produces ~23% average weight loss vs ~15% from semaglutide alone.`,
