    studies:[{title:'BPC-157 Systematic Review',journal:'Orthopaedic Journal of Sports Medicine 2025',summary:'36-study review. Enhanced angiogenesis and tissue repair. 7/12 patients reported joint pain relief.',pmid:'',url:'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12313605/'}],
    plainEnglish:`Think of an injury as a section of the building that's collapsed — a wall down, a support beam cracked, a floor buckled.\n\nTwo completely separate repair processes need to happen: the local crew has to fix the specific site, and the building's structural framework has to stay maintained so nothing else fails while the main repair is underway.\n\nMost people only address one. This stack handles both.\n\n**BPC-157 — The Local Repair Crew**\n\nGoes directly to the damage address and does the first job: promotes new blood vessel growth at the injury site. No blood supply means no delivery of repair materials. No materials means no healing. BPC-157 runs the supply lines to exactly where they're needed. Inject near the injury for maximum effect.\n\n**TB-500 — The Structural Engineer**\n\nWhile BPC-157 handles the specific address, TB-500 works on the whole building simultaneously — regulating actin throughout the entire body, reducing scar tissue formation, and maintaining structural integrity everywhere while the main repair is happening.\n\n| Compound | Role |\n|---|---|\n| BPC-157 | Local repair crew — runs supply lines directly to the damage site |\n| TB-500 | Structural engineer — keeps the whole building sound while repairs run |\n\n---\n\nBPC-157 fixes the address. TB-500 keeps the rest of the building from degrading while it does. Every serious healing protocol uses both because local repair and systemic structural maintenance are two completely different jobs — and both need to happen for a full recovery.`,
